From 5a5e9a0c5e8013b31886b6569880dc471c3f4676 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 24bit192kHz <24bit192khz@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 22:37:54 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 02/15] mb/lenovo/sklkbl_thinkpad: Power the dGPU before FSP-S

The dGPU sits behind PCIe root port 1, and FSP-S disables root ports
that have no link. dgpu_detect() powered the GPU in the ramstage, after
FspSiliconInit, when root port 1 was gone already. The GPU could only
show up after a warm reset, as its pads keep their state, which matches
the comment about the sequence working "sometimes".

Mark root port 1 as hot-pluggable, so FSP-S keeps it without a link,
and power the GPU and release its reset right before FSP-S. Root port 1
uses CLKREQ0#, and that pad has to be in its native function for the PCH
to drive the reference clock, so configure it there, too. Wait for power
good with a timeout instead of forever; on the T480 it takes 4.7 ms.
dgpu_detect() runs after FSP-S and before the PCI scan and waits for the
link, for up to the 100 ms that PCIe gives a device after its reset. If
there is no link, power the GPU off.

The power and reset pads are in the RSMRST well and keep their state in
S3 to S5. Remove them from the GPIO tables of the ramstage, which would
turn the GPU off before FSP-S, and turn the GPU off in the SMI handler
before the system goes to sleep.

TEST=Lenovo ThinkPad T480 with GeForce MX150 (10de:1d10), this sequence
     in a local tree that reads a CMOS byte in place of the option:
     the link is down before FSP-S and up after it (0 ms into the
     wait); the GPU is at 01:00.0 with an 8 GT/s x4 link after a cold
     boot, a warm reset, the first boot after enabling it (no extra
     reset) and S3 resume; disabled, the power rail stays off and the
     empty port stays. Linux binds pciehp to the port; the NVIDIA
     driver and five D3cold cycles of the GPU work.
     Not booted on top of main. T480s and T580 are build tested only.

Change-Id: I84264d1f3834204a5b8167ae348325697159020c
Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: 24bit192kHz <24bit192khz@proton.me>
